Coeliac Disease (Neurological Phenotype)
Complex Fatigue Entities
Gluten ataxia and peripheral neuropathy

VISIT CONTEXT

Case ID: CFE-010
Reason for Visit: Urgent evaluation of acute functional decline, severe unsteadiness, and distressing peripheral neuropathy.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient and spouse
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage indicated severe, rapid-onset gait instability and debilitating brain fog, creating an immediate and high risk of falls in the home. An urgent physical examination was required to rule out acute cerebrovascular accident (stroke) and to stabilize the patient's neurological symptoms prior to standard general practice hours.

SUBJECTIVE

Chief Complaint: I can't walk straight, my brain is completely blank, and my feet are burning like fire.
History of Present Illness: Patient with a known history of gluten sensitivity reports a 48-hour history of acute neurological deterioration following suspected hidden gluten ingestion. The patient experiences severe gait instability, profound cognitive slowing (brain fog), and ascending distal numbness without severe GI distress. The burning neuropathy in the feet is worsening, and the ataxia prevents safe mobilization to the toilet.
Functional Status: Temporarily mobility-impaired, requiring two-person assist to walk safely.
Social/Safety Concerns: Extremely high fall risk tonight. Spouse is present but physically unable to bear patient's weight if a fall occurs.

OBJECTIVE

Vitals: BP: 125/80 mmHg | HR: 82 bpm | Temp: 36.6 C | RR: 14 /min | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Alert but slow to respond, frustrated by cognitive limitations, visibly unsteady when attempting to stand.
Physical Exam: Pronounced broad-based, ataxic gait. Romberg test is highly positive. Distal symmetric loss of pinprick sensation in both feet, extending to the ankles. Deep tendon reflexes are diminished at the ankles. Cranial nerves grossly intact; no unilateral weakness or facial droop. Findings map to acute gluten ataxia and peripheral neuropathy.
Environmental Observations: Loose rugs in the living room; bathroom is poorly lit. Immediate fall hazards identified.
Medication Reconciliation: No recent changes to medications. No known nephrotoxic or neurotoxic drug exposures.

ASSESSMENT

Primary Diagnosis: Coeliac Disease (Neurological Phenotype) - Status: Exacerbated
Differentials:

Acute ischemic stroke (cerebellar) - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Bilateral symmetrical presentation, lack of nystagmus, and absence of cranial nerve deficits.

Guillain-Barré Syndrome - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Preservation of proximal muscle strength and lack of preceding severe viral illness.

B12 deficiency neuropathy - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Acute nature of this specific flare tied to dietary exposure, though chronic subclinical deficiency remains a long-term differential.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: High

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY

The sudden onset of marked ataxia and cognitive dysfunction closely mimics an acute cerebellar or posterior circulation stroke, mandating an immediate, face-to-face neurological assessment. The after-hours visit was critical to formally exclude acute cerebrovascular pathology through targeted cranial nerve and motor examination. Delaying this assessment would have left a high-risk patient exposed to potentially devastating falls and diagnostic uncertainty regarding a time-critical stroke.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN

Clinical Interventions: Commenced Amitriptyline 10mg nocte for acute neuropathic pain modulation. Targeted intervention strategy includes PEA, B6, and LDN. Ordered comprehensive neuro-immunology and nutritional blood panels (B12, Folate, Iron) for routine GP follow-up.
Positive Behavioral Change: By strictly utilizing a walking aid and seeking assistance for all transfers tonight, you will physically prevent a traumatic fall, rewarding your caution with continued physical safety while the ataxia resolves.
Home-Based Services (Optional): Referral for occupational therapy home safety assessment.
DME/Safety (Optional): Provision of a temporary four-wheel walker; removal of loose rugs advised.
Follow-up: Routine GP review in 48 hours to assess gait stability and initiate nutritional/neurological blood panels.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN

Immediate transfer to Emergency Department via 000 if patient develops unilateral weakness, facial droop, slurred speech, or difficulty swallowing.

PATIENT INFORMATION

Disclaimer: Neurological symptoms of gluten exposure can be prolonged; complete resolution requires absolute, uncompromising dietary adherence over months.
Clinical Course: The acute ataxia will likely plateau and slowly recede over the next 7-14 days. Neuropathic pain will respond partially to medication but requires time.

SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE

Call 000 immediately if you suddenly cannot speak clearly, if one side of your face drops, or if you completely lose the ability to move an arm or leg.

PATIENT EDUCATION

Treatment Explanation: The neurological reaction to gluten causes acute inflammation in the nerves and balance centers of the brain; the new medication helps quiet the inflamed nerve endings in your feet to allow sleep.
Actionable Steps: Clear all immediate trip hazards and strictly use the prescribed walking frame tomorrow; mitigating the physical risk directly prevents secondary injury, allowing the brain time to clear the neuroinflammation.

